INSERT INTO _s496_l1_c VALUES
(6956,1,17,$n$And he shall go before him in the spirit and power of Elijah, to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children, and the disobedient to the wisdom of the just; to make ready a people prepared for Yahuah (Lord).$n$,'malachi',4,5,$n$thread:yochanan-as-eliyahu-pattern-prophet | *And he shall go before him in the spirit and power of Eliyahu (Elijah), to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children, and the disobedient to the wisdom of the just; to make ready a people prepared for Yahuah (the Lord).* The angel quotes Mal'aki (Malachi) 4:5-6 directly into Zecharyah (Zacharias)'s ear. Yochanan (John) is the Eliyahu-pattern; not Eliyahu himself, but the prophet in his spirit-and-power, doing the prepared-people work.$n$,$n$Malachi 4:5 — *Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of Yahuah (LORD):* *And he shall go before him in the spirit and power of Elijah, to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children, and the disobedient to the wisdom of the just; to make ready a people prepared for Yahuah (Lord).* (Luke 1:17) Gabriel is quoting Malachi into Zacharias's ear. The one who goes before comes *in the spirit and power of Elijah*, and Malachi had promised *Elijah the prophet* before the great and dreadful day of Yahuah (the LORD). Just before he says it, Malachi writes *Remember ye the law of Moses my servant* (Malachi 4:4), so the turning John brings is toward Moses and not away from him.$n$),
(6958,1,31,$n$And, behold, you shall conceive in your womb, and bring forth a son, and shall call his name Yahusha (JESUS).$n$,'2-samuel',7,12,$n$thread:the-seed-of-promise-is-singular-paternal | *He shall be great, and shall be called the Son of the Highest: and Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) shall give unto him the throne of his father David: and he shall reign over the house of Ya'aqov (Jacob) for ever.* The angel's word is the 2 Samuel 7 Davidic promise landing on the paternal-seed-singular — *to thy seed, which is HaMashiach (Christ).* The throne goes to ONE seed in the line of David, not to a metaphorical extension. The *house of Ya'aqov* names all twelve tribes, not a metaphorical Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,$n$2 Samuel 7:12 — *And when thy days be fulfilled, and thou shalt sleep with thy fathers, I will set up thy seed after thee, which shall proceed out of thy bowels, and I will establish his kingdom.* *And, behold, you shall conceive in your womb, and bring forth a son, and shall call his name Yahusha (JESUS).* (Luke 1:31) Gabriel tells Mary she will bear a son and call his name Yahusha (Jesus), and the word Yahuah (the LORD) gave David stands behind it. David was told *I will set up thy seed after thee*, a seed that would *proceed out of thy bowels*. One seed out of David's own body, and Mary is carrying him.$n$),
(6959,1,32,$n$He shall be great, and shall be called the Son of the Highest: and Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) shall give unto him the throne of his father David:$n$,'2-samuel',7,12,$n$thread:messianic-line-preservation-by-sovereign-decree | *He shall be great, and shall be called the Son of the Highest: and Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) shall give unto him the throne of his father David.* The 2 Samuel 7:12-13 Davidic covenant preserved through the line — the angel's naming over the unborn King secures the same paternal-seed-singular Messianic-line the Tanakh has carried under sovereign-decree from Bereshit 49:10's scepter-not-departing onward.$n$,$n$2 Samuel 7:12 — *And when thy days be fulfilled, and thou shalt sleep with thy fathers, I will set up thy seed after thee, which shall proceed out of thy bowels, and I will establish his kingdom.* *He shall be great, and shall be called the Son of the Highest: and Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) shall give unto him the throne of his father David:* (Luke 1:32) Gabriel names the throne: Yahuah (the LORD) will give him *the throne of his father David*. That is David's own promise coming due, a kingdom established for the seed after him, and the next verse of it says Yahuah (the LORD) will *stablish the throne of his kingdom for ever* (2 Samuel 7:13). The throne is not new. It is David's.$n$),
(6960,1,33,$n$And he shall reign over the house of Jacob for ever; and of his kingdom there shall be no end.$n$,'daniel',7,14,$n$thread:messianic-line-preservation-by-sovereign-decree | *And of his kingdom there shall be no end.* The Daniyel (Daniel) 7:14 *his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away, and his kingdom that which shall not be destroyed* — the angel's declaration over Miriam (Mary) names the eternal-kingdom-of-the-Son-of-Adam preserved through Davidic-line by sovereign-decree.$n$,$n$Daniel 7:14 — *And there was given him dominion, and glory, and a kingdom, that all people, nations, and languages, should serve him: his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away, and his kingdom that which shall not be destroyed.* *And he shall reign over the house of Jacob for ever; and of his kingdom there shall be no end.* (Luke 1:33) Gabriel's *for ever* and *no end* are what Daniel saw given: *his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away*, and a kingdom *that which shall not be destroyed*. Daniel says every people and language serves him. Gabriel says where the reign stands, over the house of Jacob, all twelve tribes, and not another people in its place.$n$),
(6964,1,55,$n$As he spake to our fathers, to Abraham, and to his seed for ever.$n$,'genesis',17,7,$n$thread:avraham-reckoned-before-circumcision | *As he spake to our fathers, to Avraham (Abraham), and to his seed for ever.* Miriam (Mary) is naming the everlasting covenant of Bereshit (Genesis) 17:7 — *to be a Elohim (God) unto thee, and to thy seed after thee* — and locating her son inside it as the keeping of that oath. The covenant the Reformation collapsed into a faith-confession is here named by a daughter-of-Yashar'el as a paternal-seed-promise to her fathers.$n$,$n$Genesis 17:7 — *And I will establish my covenant between me and thee and thy seed after thee in their generations for an everlasting covenant, to be a Elohim (God) unto thee, and to thy seed after thee.* *As he spake to our fathers, to Abraham, and to his seed for ever.* (Luke 1:55) Mary says it was spoken *to Abraham, and to his seed for ever*, and Genesis has the words themselves, *an everlasting covenant*, with Elohim (God) to be *a Elohim (God) unto thee, and to thy seed after thee*. Her son is the keeping of that oath to the fathers, as Zacharias says a few verses on: *The oath which he sware to our father Abraham* (Luke 1:73).$n$),
(6965,1,67,$n$And his father Zacharias was filled with the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit), and prophesied, saying,$n$,'isaiah',11,1,$n$thread:gospel-promised-afore | *Blessed be Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) of Yashar'el (Israel); for he hath visited and redeemed his people, and hath raised up an horn of salvation for us in the house of his servant David; as he spake by the mouth of his holy prophets, which have been since the world began.* Zecharyah (Zacharias) names exactly what *gospel-promised-afore* names — the redemption is *as he spake by the mouth of his holy prophets,* the horn-of-salvation rising in the house of David is the Yeshayahu (Isaiah) 11:1 branch out of the stem of Yishai (Jesse).$n$,$n$Isaiah 11:1 — *And there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots:* *And his father Zacharias was filled with the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit), and prophesied, saying,* (Luke 1:67) Zacharias is filled with the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit), and what he prophesies is *he hath visited and redeemed his people, and hath raised up an horn of salvation for us in the house of his servant David* (Luke 1:68-69). Isaiah had the same shoot in another picture, *a rod out of the stem of Jesse*, and Jesse is David's father.$n$),
(6970,2,13,$n$And suddenly there was with the angel a multitude of the heavenly host praising Elohim (God), and saying,$n$,'revelation',19,1,$n$thread:trumpets-and-the-day-of-yahuah | *And suddenly there was with the angel a multitude of the heavenly host praising Elohim (God), and saying, Glory to Elohim (God) in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men.* The angelic multitude at the birth is the same heavenly hosts that praise around the throne in Revelation 19 — the heavens stand witness at the moment the Formed-one steps into flesh, just as they will stand witness at his return.$n$,$n$Revelation 19:1 — *And after these things I heard a great voice of much people in heaven, saying, Alleluia; Salvation, and glory, and honour, and power, unto Yahuah Eloheinu (the Lord our God):* *And suddenly there was with the angel a multitude of the heavenly host praising Elohim (God), and saying,* (Luke 2:13) At the birth in Luke, a *multitude of the heavenly host* is praising Elohim (God) over the shepherds' field. Revelation has heaven full of voices again: *a great voice of much people in heaven*, saying *Alleluia; Salvation, and glory, and honour, and power* to Elohim (God). Both times the sky gives the glory to Elohim (God), and the second time it is because *true and righteous are his judgments* (Revelation 19:2).$n$),
(6973,3,2,$n$Annas and Caiaphas being the high priests, the word of Elohim (God) came unto John the son of Zacharias in the wilderness.$n$,'isaiah',40,3,$n$thread:yochanan-as-eliyahu-pattern-prophet | Luke quotes Yeshayahu (Isaiah) 40:3 explicitly — *The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of Yahuah (the Lord), make his paths straight.* Yochanan's (John's) Eliyahu-pattern (Elijah-pattern) prophet ministry begins where the Tanakh said it would. Same scripture, same wilderness, same voice.$n$,$n$Isaiah 40:3 — *The voice of him that crieth in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of Yahuah (LORD), make straight in the desert a highway for our Elohim (God).* *Annas and Caiaphas being the high priests, the word of Elohim (God) came unto John the son of Zacharias in the wilderness.* (Luke 3:2) Luke dates the hour by emperors and high priests, and then says *the word of Elohim (God) came unto John* in the wilderness. Isaiah had put a voice in that very place, *him that crieth in the wilderness*, with the call *Prepare ye the way of Yahuah (LORD)*, and Luke goes on to hang Isaiah's words on John. The word arrives where Isaiah said the crying would be.$n$),
(6976,3,8,$n$Bring forth therefore fruits worthy of repentance, and begin not to say within yourselves, We have Abraham to our father: for I say unto you, That Elohim (God) is able of these stones to raise up children unto Abraham.$n$,'matthew',3,9,$n$thread:another-gospel-as-judaizer-flesh-credential | *And begin not to say within yourselves, We have Avraham (Abraham) to our father: for I say unto you, that Elohim (God) is able of these stones to raise up children unto Avraham (Abraham).* Yochanan (John) rebukes the Pharisaic flesh-credential gospel — being born a Yahudi (Jew) saves no one whose heart is not refined.$n$,$n$Matthew 3:9 — *And think not to say within yourselves, We have Abraham to our father: for I say unto you, that Elohim (God) is able of these stones to raise up children unto Abraham.* *Bring forth therefore fruits worthy of repentance, and begin not to say within yourselves, We have Abraham to our father: for I say unto you, That Elohim (God) is able of these stones to raise up children unto Abraham.* (Luke 3:8) Luke says John spoke to *the multitude that came forth to be baptized* (Luke 3:7), where Matthew names Pharisees and Sadducees. Either way fruit comes first, and no one hides behind *We have Abraham to our father*. The stones read well beside Hosea: *Ye are the sons of the living Elohim (God)* (Hosea 1:10), said in the place where they were not his people.$n$),
(6984,4,5,$n$And the devil, taking him up into an high mountain, shewed unto him all the kingdoms of the world in a moment of time.$n$,'matthew',4,8,$n$thread:kingdoms-now-under-watcher-rebellion-administration | *And the devil, taking him up into an high mountain, shewed unto him all the kingdoms of the world in a moment of time. And the devil said unto him, All this power will I give thee, and the glory of them: for that is delivered unto me; and to whomsoever I will I give it.* Luke uniquely records the satan saying *that is delivered unto me* — the kingdoms-now-under-watcher-rebellion-administration substance is named by the satan's own mouth.$n$,$n$Matthew 4:8 — *Again, the devil taketh him up into an exceeding high mountain, and sheweth him all the kingdoms of the world, and the glory of them;* *And the devil, taking him up into an high mountain, shewed unto him all the kingdoms of the world in a moment of time.* (Luke 4:5) Matthew has the mountain *exceeding high* and the devil showing *all the kingdoms of the world, and the glory of them*. Luke has the same sight *in a moment of time*, and lets the devil say why he can offer it: *for that is delivered unto me; and to whomsoever I will I give it* (Luke 4:6). In Luke the devil himself says the kingdoms have been put into his hand.$n$),
(6987,4,25,$n$But I tell you of a truth, many widows were in Yashar'el (Israel) in the days of Elijah, when the heaven was shut up three years and six months, when great famine was throughout all the land;$n$,'matthew',15,24,$n$thread:canaanite-woman-master | The King at Natsareth (Nazareth) points to Eliyahu (Elijah) and the widow of Tsarephath (Zarephath), and Elisha and Naaman the Syrian — the individual-mercy contrast cases the framework holds against the false-inclusion-gospel. These are foreign-origin individuals receiving mercy without absorption into the seed-of-promise.$n$,$n$Matthew 15:24 — *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* *But I tell you of a truth, many widows were in Yashar'el (Israel) in the days of Elijah, when the heaven was shut up three years and six months, when great famine was throughout all the land;* (Luke 4:25) At Nazareth Yahusha (Jesus) says *unto none of them was Elijah sent, save unto Sarepta, a city of Sidon* (Luke 4:26), and that none was cleansed *saving Naaman the Syrian* (Luke 4:27). Beside *the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)*, they are sheep of that house who heard a prophet where it was scattered. The synagogue rose against him for saying the choosing is the Father's.$n$),
(6989,4,43,$n$And he said unto them, I must preach the kingdom of Elohim (God) to other cities also: for therefore am I sent.$n$,'matthew',15,24,$n$thread:lost-sheep-mission-scope | *I must preach the kingdom of Elohim (God) to other cities also: for therefore am I sent.* The King's *therefore am I sent* names the mission scope — to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), city by city, through the Galilean territory where the scattered live.$n$,$n$Matthew 15:24 — *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* *And he said unto them, I must preach the kingdom of Elohim (God) to other cities also: for therefore am I sent.* (Luke 4:43) Matthew says whom he was sent to: *the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)*. Luke shows what that errand looks like on the road. The town would have kept him, and he says he must preach in *other cities also*, *for therefore am I sent*. The sending moves him from city to city after those sheep.$n$),
(6991,5,14,$n$And he charged him to tell no man: but go, and shew thyself to the priest, and offer for your cleansing, according as Moses commanded, for a testimony unto them.$n$,'matthew',8,4,$n$thread:messiah-as-torah-affirming-healer | *And he charged him to tell no man: but go, and shew thyself to the priest, and offer for thy cleansing, according as Mosheh (Moses) commanded, for a testimony unto them.* The King sends the cleansed leper to the priest with the Vayikra (Leviticus) 14 offering — the same Torah-affirming move Matthew 8:4 carries.$n$,$n$Matthew 8:4 — *And Yahusha (Jesus) saith unto him, See thou tell no man; but go thy way, shew thyself to the priest, and offer the gift that Moses commanded, for a testimony unto them.* *And he charged him to tell no man: but go, and shew thyself to the priest, and offer for your cleansing, according as Moses commanded, for a testimony unto them.* (Luke 5:14) Both gospels send the cleansed man to the priest, and both say it is Moses' commandment and not a new one. Matthew calls it *the gift that Moses commanded*. Luke says what the offering is for: *offer for thy cleansing, according as Moses commanded*. Yahusha (Jesus) touches the leper and then sends him straight back into the Torah's own order for a cleansed leper.$n$),
(6994,5,34,$n$And he said unto them, Can you make the children of the bridechamber fast, while the bridegroom is with them?$n$,'matthew',9,15,$n$thread:bridegroom-messianic-identification | *Can ye make the children of the bridechamber fast, while the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken away from them, and then shall they fast in those days.* The King's bridegroom-self-identification names what Yeshayahu (Isaiah) 62:5 and Hoshea (Hosea) 2:19 already named — Yahuah (the LORD) is the bridegroom of his people.$n$,$n$Matthew 9:15 — *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Can the children of the bridechamber mourn, as long as the bridegroom is with them? but the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken from them, and then shall they fast.* *And he said unto them, Can you make the children of the bridechamber fast, while the bridegroom is with them?* (Luke 5:34) Matthew asks whether the wedding guests can *mourn* while the bridegroom is with them, and Luke asks whether you can make them *fast*. It is the same question put to the ones who asked about fasting. Either way he sits in the bridegroom's place, and Isaiah gives that place to Elohim (God): *as the bridegroom rejoiceth over the bride, so shall thy Elohim (God) rejoice over thee* (Isaiah 62:5).$n$),
(6995,5,36,$n$And he spake also a parable unto them; No man puts a piece of a new garment upon an old; if otherwise, then both the new makes a rent, and the piece that was taken out of the new agreeth not with the old.$n$,'matthew',9,17,$n$thread:leaven-as-corruption-substance | *Neither do men put new wine into old bottles; else the new wine will burst the bottles, and be spilled, and the bottles shall perish. But new wine must be put into new bottles; and both are preserved.* The new-wine-and-old-bottles substance is the new-heart-and-new-spirit Yechezk'el (Ezekiel) 36:26-27 substance. The King's *new wine* is not new Torah; it is the new heart that holds the same Torah without bursting.$n$,$n$Matthew 9:17 — *Neither do men put new wine into old bottles: else the bottles break, and the wine runneth out, and the bottles perish: but they put new wine into new bottles, and both are preserved.* *And he spake also a parable unto them; No man puts a piece of a new garment upon an old; if otherwise, then both the new makes a rent, and the piece that was taken out of the new agreeth not with the old.* (Luke 5:36) Luke has a piece of new cloth on an old garment, and Matthew's verse has *new wine into old bottles*. It is one saying told with two figures. Put the new into something too worn to hold it and the garment tears and the bottles burst, but *they put new wine into new bottles, and both are preserved*. Both figures are about what a vessel can hold.$n$),
(6996,6,1,$n$And it came to pass on the second sabbath after the first, that he went through the corn fields; and his disciples plucked the ears of corn, and did eat, rubbing them in their hands.$n$,'mark',2,23,$n$thread:shabbat-controversy-heart-shabbat-vs-pharisaic-fence | The grain-plucking Shabbat controversy in Luke runs the same David-and-the-shewbread argument Mark 2:23-28 and Matthew 12:1-8 carry. *The Son of Adam is Lord also of the Shabbat.*$n$,$n$Mark 2:23 — *And it came to pass, that he went through the corn fields on the sabbath day; and his disciples began, as they went, to pluck the ears of corn.* *And it came to pass on the second sabbath after the first, that he went through the corn fields; and his disciples plucked the ears of corn, and did eat, rubbing them in their hands.* (Luke 6:1) Mark has them going *through the corn fields on the sabbath day*. Luke dates it, *the second sabbath after the first*, and says they rubbed the ears in their hands and ate. That is a hungry man's meal, and Moses left it open: *thou mayest pluck the ears with thine hand* (Deuteronomy 23:25). The day stands in both tellings, and the charge is about a fence laid over it.$n$),
(6997,6,6,$n$And it came to pass also on another sabbath, that he entered into the synagogue and taught: and there was a man whose right hand was withered.$n$,'mark',3,1,$n$thread:shabbat-controversy-heart-shabbat-vs-pharisaic-fence | The withered-hand healing on Shabbat — *Is it lawful on the Shabbat to do good, or to do evil? to save life, or to destroy it?* — is the same heart-Shabbat-versus-Pharisaic-fence move Mark 3 and Matthew 12 carry. Luke uniquely closes with *they were filled with madness* (v.11), naming the Pharisees' fury at the heart-Torah override of their fence-system.$n$,$n$Mark 3:1 — *And he entered again into the synagogue; and there was a man there which had a withered hand.* *And it came to pass also on another sabbath, that he entered into the synagogue and taught: and there was a man whose right hand was withered.* (Luke 6:6) Mark says he *entered again into the synagogue*, and Luke tells which day and what he was doing: *another sabbath*, and he *taught*. Luke also says the man's *right hand was withered*. The day is kept in both, in the synagogue with the withered hand in front of him, and Luke closes the scene with the watchers *filled with madness* (Luke 6:11).$n$),
(6998,6,12,$n$And it came to pass in those days, that he went out into a mountain to pray, and continued all night in prayer to Elohim (God).$n$,'matthew',10,1,$n$thread:twelve-apostles-twelve-tribes-restoration-architecture | *And it came to pass in those days, that he went out into a mountain to pray, and continued all night in prayer to Elohim (God). And when it was day, he called unto him his disciples: and of them he chose twelve, whom also he named apostles.* The choosing of the twelve is the twelve-tribes-restoration architecture. Luke uniquely notes the all-night prayer that precedes the choosing.$n$,$n$Matthew 10:1 — *And when he had called unto him his twelve disciples, he gave them power against unclean spirits, to cast them out, and to heal all manner of sickness and all manner of disease.* *And it came to pass in those days, that he went out into a mountain to pray, and continued all night in prayer to Elohim (God).* (Luke 6:12) Matthew has the twelve already called and given power. Luke shows what came first: a night on a mountain, *all night in prayer to Elohim (God)*, and at day he chose twelve. He tells these same men what the number is for, *sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel)* (Luke 22:30), so the choosing began in prayer and the number points at the tribes.$n$),
(7006,7,9,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard these things, he marvelled at him, and turned him about, and said unto the people that followed him, I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel).$n$,'matthew',8,10,$n$thread:my-brethren-substance-distinguishing-the-seed-of-promise-from-the-categorical-nations | *I have not found so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel).* The verdict frames the centurion's faith against the seed-of-promise body — the framework's individual-mercy carveout per Red Line #7. The Gentile centurion receives mercy without absorption; the seed-of-promise body remains the audience of the gathering.$n$,$n$Matthew 8:10 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard it, he marvelled, and said to them that followed, Verily I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel).* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard these things, he marvelled at him, and turned him about, and said unto the people that followed him, I say unto you, I have not found so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel).* (Luke 7:9) The words are the same in both gospels. Luke adds who stood around the soldier: the elders of the Yahudim (Jews) went first, saying *he loveth our nation, and he hath built us a synagogue* (Luke 7:5), and he sent friends to say *I am not worthy* (Luke 7:6). He knew what a word can do, and Yahusha (Jesus) says of this soldier what he had not found in the house at home, *no, not in Yashar'el (Israel)*: a sheep who heard, out where the house was scattered.$n$),
(7007,7,1,$n$Now when he had ended all his sayings in the audience of the people, he entered into Capernaum.$n$,'matthew',8,5,$n$thread:canaanite-woman-master | The centurion at Capernaum is the parallel individual-mercy case to the Canaanite woman at Matthew 15 / Mark 7 — a Gentile-origin individual receiving mercy without absorption into the seed-of-promise. (Cross-witness on canaanite-woman-master; primary thread is my-brethren-substance per verifier.)$n$,$n$Matthew 8:5 — *And when Yahusha (Jesus) was entered into Capernaum, there came unto him a centurion, beseeching him,* *Now when he had ended all his sayings in the audience of the people, he entered into Capernaum.* (Luke 7:1) Both gospels bring Yahusha (Jesus) into Capernaum and put the centurion before him, but they tell the coming differently. In Matthew the centurion comes *beseeching him*. In Luke the entry follows the end of all his sayings in the hearing of the people, and the soldier sends first the elders of the Yahudim (Jews) and then friends, so that he never stands before him himself.$n$),
(7008,7,27,$n$This is he, of whom it is written, Behold, I send my messenger before your face, which shall prepare your way before you.$n$,'malachi',3,1,$n$thread:yochanan-as-eliyahu-pattern-prophet | *This is he, of whom it is written, Behold, I send my messenger before thy face, which shall prepare thy way before thee.* The King's own Mal'aki (Malachi) 3:1 attribution of Yochanan (John) as the Eliyahu (Elijah)-pattern messenger.$n$,$n$Malachi 3:1 — *Behold, I will send my messenger, and he shall prepare the way before me: and Yahuah (Lord), whom ye seek, shall suddenly come to his temple, even the messenger of the covenant, whom ye delight in: behold, he shall come, saith Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts).* *This is he, of whom it is written, Behold, I send my messenger before your face, which shall prepare your way before you.* (Luke 7:27) Yahusha (Jesus) says *This is he, of whom it is written*, and the writing is Malachi. *My messenger* is sent to *prepare the way before me*, and the same verse says who comes down that road: Yahuah (the Lord) *whom ye seek*, who *shall suddenly come to his temple*. John is the one sent ahead, and Malachi makes plain that someone greater follows him.$n$),
(7009,7,28,$n$For I say unto you, Among those that are born of women there is not a greater prophet than John the Baptist: but he that is least in the kingdom of Elohim (God) is greater than he.$n$,'matthew',11,11,$n$thread:yochanan-as-eliyahu-pattern-prophet | *Among those that are born of women there is not a greater prophet than Yochanan (John) the Baptist: but he that is least in the kingdom of Elohim (God) is greater than he.* The synoptic parallel at Matthew 11:11 — the King's verdict on Yochanan as the Eliyahu-pattern messenger; the *least in the kingdom is greater* names the architecture of the new-covenant restoration giving those who enter the kingdom a covenant-position Yochanan's prophetic office did not occupy.$n$,$n$Matthew 11:11 — *Verily I say unto you, Among them that are born of women there hath not risen a greater than John the Baptist: notwithstanding he that is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.* *For I say unto you, Among those that are born of women there is not a greater prophet than John the Baptist: but he that is least in the kingdom of Elohim (God) is greater than he.* (Luke 7:28) Both gospels give the same measure of John, the greatest of those born of women, and the same turn after it, that *he that is least in the kingdom* is greater. Luke names the kind of greatness, *a greater prophet*. Matthew's next verses say what that prophet was: *all the prophets and the law prophesied until John* (Matthew 11:13), and *this is Elijah, which was for to come* (Matthew 11:14).$n$),
(7010,7,29,$n$And all the people that heard him, and the publicans, justified Elohim (God), being baptized with the baptism of John.$n$,'john',10,27,$n$thread:yochanan-as-eliyahu-pattern-prophet | *And all the people that heard him, and the publicans, justified Elohim (God), being baptized with the baptism of Yochanan (John). But the Pharisees and lawyers rejected the counsel of Elohim (God) against themselves, being not baptized of him.* The hearing-and-turning the King names — *my sheep hear my voice* (John 10:27). The publicans heard; the system-credentialed rejected.$n$,$n$John 10:27 — *My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me:* *And all the people that heard him, and the publicans, justified Elohim (God), being baptized with the baptism of John.* (Luke 7:29) Luke says the people and the publicans who heard John *justified Elohim (God)*, and in the next verse the Pharisees and lawyers *rejected the counsel of Elohim (God) against themselves* (Luke 7:30). The same preaching, two answers. *My sheep hear my voice*, Yahusha (Jesus) says, and the hearing shows who they were. It does not make a sheep of what was not one.$n$),
(7011,7,33,$n$For John the Baptist came neither eating bread nor drinking wine; and you say, He has a devil.$n$,'1-kings',19,8,$n$thread:yochanan-as-eliyahu-pattern-prophet | *For Yochanan (John) the Baptist came neither eating bread nor drinking wine.* The Eliyahu (Elijah)-pattern ascetic-register the King names as Yochanan's signature, matching Eliyahu's wilderness-fasting at 1 Melakhim (Kings) 19:8.$n$,$n$1 Kings 19:8 — *And he arose, and did eat and drink, and went in the strength of that meat forty days and forty nights unto Horeb the mount of Elohim (God).* *For John the Baptist came neither eating bread nor drinking wine; and you say, He has a devil.* (Luke 7:33) Elijah ate what the angel gave him and *in the strength of that meat forty days and forty nights* he went to Horeb. John comes out of the same kind of wilderness *neither eating bread nor drinking wine*, and the people who watched him said *He hath a devil*. Elijah's road and John's are the same road through the same empty country.$n$),
(7012,7,24,$n$And when the messengers of John were departed, he began to speak unto the people concerning John, What went you out into the wilderness for to see? A reed shaken with the wind?$n$,'1-kings',19,10,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | The reed-shaken-with-the-wind verdict the King refuses on Yochanan (John)'s behalf — Yochanan is the Eliyahu (Elijah)-pattern prophet, including the prophetic-martyrdom register Eliyahu walked under Yzevel (Jezebel).$n$,$n$1 Kings 19:10 — *And he said, I have been very jealous for Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) of hosts: for the children of Yashar'el (Israel) have forsaken thy covenant, thrown down thine altars, and slain thy prophets with the sword; and I, even I only, am left; and they seek my life, to take it away.* *And when the messengers of John were departed, he began to speak unto the people concerning John, What went you out into the wilderness for to see? A reed shaken with the wind?* (Luke 7:24) Yahusha (Jesus) asks the crowd what they went out to see, and it was not *a reed shaken with the wind*. It was a prophet, and Elijah had said in his cave what that costs: *thy covenant* forsaken, the altars thrown down, the prophets slain, and *they seek my life*. John stands in that same place in the wilderness, speaking the covenant to a house that had let it go.$n$),
(7014,8,4,$n$And when much people were gathered together, and were come to him out of every city, he spake by a parable:$n$,'matthew',13,3,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | The parable of the sower in Luke's register — same architecture as Matthew 13 / Mark 4. The four soils walk the cause-and-effect-of-hearing diagnostic: the seed is one, the soil reveals what is already true of each hearer.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:3 — *And he spake many things unto them in parables, saying, Behold, a sower went forth to sow;* *And when much people were gathered together, and were come to him out of every city, he spake by a parable:* (Luke 8:4) Matthew has him *spake many things unto them in parables* and begins with the sower. Luke says who was there to hear: *much people* gathered *out of every city*. The seed goes out to everyone alike, a whole country of hearers, and the parable that follows is about what the ground shows of each one when the same word lands on it.$n$),
(7015,8,10,$n$And he said, Unto you it is given to know the mysteries of the kingdom of Elohim (God): but to others in parables; that seeing they might not see, and hearing they might not understand.$n$,'matthew',13,11,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | *Unto you it is given to know the mysteries of the kingdom of Elohim (God): but to others in parables; that seeing they might not see, and hearing they might not understand.* The cause-and-effect-of-hearing the King states explicitly — the parables are not pedagogy designed to clarify; they are the mechanism by which the Father reveals to the elect.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:11 — *He answered and said unto them, Because it is given unto you to know the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven, but to them it is not given.* *And he said, Unto you it is given to know the mysteries of the kingdom of Elohim (God): but to others in parables; that seeing they might not see, and hearing they might not understand.* (Luke 8:10) Both gospels say it *is given* to the disciples to know the mysteries, and not to the others. Matthew has them ask why he speaks in parables, and Luke has them ask what this parable might be. The giving is named first and the hearing follows it, and Luke keeps Yahusha's (Jesus's) own words that the others are left *seeing they might not see*.$n$),
(7017,8,21,$n$And he answered and said unto them, My mother and my brethren are these which hear the word of Elohim (God), and do it.$n$,'matthew',12,50,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | *My mother and my brethren are these which hear the word of Elohim (God), and do it.* The framework-substance: hearing-and-doing reveals the family. The cause-and-effect-of-hearing diagnostic in compressed form.$n$,$n$Matthew 12:50 — *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* *And he answered and said unto them, My mother and my brethren are these which hear the word of Elohim (God), and do it.* (Luke 8:21) Matthew says it by the Father's will, *the will of my Father which is in heaven*, and Luke says it by the Father's word: *these which hear the word of Elohim (God), and do it*. Same household, and what shows it is the doing. His mother and brothers were outside and could not get near him for the crowd, and he points to those who hear the word and keep it.$n$),
(7022,9,5,$n$And whosoever will not receive you, when you go out of that city, shake off the very dust from your feet for a testimony against them.$n$,'matthew',10,14,$n$thread:shake-the-dust-off-rejection-response | *Whosoever will not receive you, when ye go out of that city, shake off the very dust from your feet for a testimony against them.* The framework's reading: the pursuit ends where the rejection lands; we do not chase the nations into a worship they will not give.$n$,$n$Matthew 10:14 — *And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear your words, when ye depart out of that house or city, shake off the dust of your feet.* *And whosoever will not receive you, when you go out of that city, shake off the very dust from your feet for a testimony against them.* (Luke 9:5) Luke tells what the dust is for. Matthew has the town that will not *receive you, nor hear your words*, and Luke's *for a testimony against them* says the shaking is a witness left standing at the door. The twelve are not sent after the town to argue it round; the word has been spoken, and they go out of the city.$n$),
(7023,9,10,$n$And the apostles, when they were returned, told him all that they had done. And he took them, and went aside privately into a desert place belonging to the city called Bethsaida.$n$,'matthew',14,13,$n$thread:wilderness-feeding-as-messianic-banquet-pre-figuration | The feeding of the five thousand in Luke's register — the same wilderness-feeding-as-messianic-banquet substance with the twelve baskets answering the twelve tribes.$n$,$n$Matthew 14:13 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard of it, he departed thence by ship into a desert place apart: and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* *And the apostles, when they were returned, told him all that they had done. And he took them, and went aside privately into a desert place belonging to the city called Bethsaida.* (Luke 9:10) Luke has the apostles just back and telling him *all that they had done*, and he takes them aside to a desert place by Bethsaida. Matthew says what sent him there, the news of John's death, and that the people *followed him on foot out of the cities*. So the lonely place is already filling with a crowd, and the twelve he has just taken aside will soon be the ones asked to feed it.$n$),
(7024,9,18,$n$And it came to pass, as he was alone praying, his disciples were with him: and he asked them, saying, Whom say the people that I am?$n$,'matthew',16,13,$n$thread:caesarea-philippi-confession-the-rock-the-keys-the-assembly | Luke renders Kefa's (Peter's) confession in a quieter register — *the Mashiach (Christ) of Elohim (God)* (Luke 9:20). Same architecture as Matthew 16 / Mark 8.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:13 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) came into the coasts of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, saying, Whom do men say that I the Son of Adam am?* *And it came to pass, as he was alone praying, his disciples were with him: and he asked them, saying, Whom say the people that I am?* (Luke 9:18) Luke has him praying alone with the disciples near, and he asks *Whom say the people that I am?* with no place named. Matthew puts the same question at Caesarea Philippi and has him ask it with the title already in his mouth, *the Son of Adam*. What Luke gives next is Peter's answer, *The Messiah (Christ) of Elohim (God)* (Luke 9:20), and nothing is said of a rock or keys.$n$),
(7025,9,21,$n$And he straitly charged them, and commanded them to tell no man that thing;$n$,'matthew',16,21,$n$thread:first-passion-prediction-and-the-must-of-the-passion | *The Son of Adam must suffer many things... and be slain, and be raised the third day.* The first-passion-prediction in Luke's register — the *must* (Greek *dei*) of the suffering, the Yeshayahu (Isaiah) 53 / Tehillim (Psalm) 22 architecture the King is walking toward consciously.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:21 — *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* *And he straitly charged them, and commanded them to tell no man that thing;* (Luke 9:21) Luke gives no gap. The order to *tell no man that thing* is followed at once by what the Son of Adam must go through, *and be slain, and be raised the third day* (Luke 9:22), where Matthew marks the turn with *From that time forth* and names Jerusalem as the place. The one just named Messiah (Christ) is told in the same hour what he must suffer.$n$),
(7026,9,23,$n$And he said to them all, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross daily, and follow me.$n$,'matthew',16,24,$n$thread:take-up-the-cross-discipleship-cost | *If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross daily, and follow me.* Luke alone adds *daily* — the cross is a daily walking, not a one-time-transaction.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:24 — *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* *And he said to them all, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross daily, and follow me.* (Luke 9:23) Luke has him say it *to them all*, where Matthew has him speak *unto his disciples*, so the saying is open to the whole company that was listening. And Luke alone adds *daily*. The cross is taken up for the length of the walk, not once.$n$),
(7027,9,28,$n$And it came to pass about an eight days after these sayings, he took Peter and John and James, and went up into a mountain to pray.$n$,'matthew',17,1,$n$thread:the-formless-father-and-the-formed-son-witnessed-in-ascension-of-isaiah | The Lukan Transfiguration parallel — Mosheh (Moses) and Eliyahu (Elijah) appearing in glory, the Father-voice naming *This is my beloved Son: hear him* (Luke 9:35). The Formless-Father / Formed-Son architecture witnessed in the Ascension of Isaiah 9-10 seventh-heaven framing.$n$,$n$Matthew 17:1 — *And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) taketh Peter, James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into an high mountain apart,* *And it came to pass about an eight days after these sayings, he took Peter and John and James, and went up into a mountain to pray.* (Luke 9:28) Luke counts *about an eight days* after the sayings where Matthew says *after six days*, and Luke says why he went up: *to pray*. In Luke the glory comes as he prays, and then the Father's voice speaks over him, *This is my beloved Son: hear him* (Luke 9:35), the voice of the unseen Father over the Son the three men can see.$n$),
(7029,9,32,$n$But Peter and they that were with him were heavy with sleep: and when they were awake, they saw his glory, and the two men that stood with him.$n$,'matthew',17,2,$n$thread:the-son-of-adam-glorified-in-1-enoch-throne-vision-and-parables | *They saw his glory, and the two men that stood with him.* The Lukan emphasis on the glory of the Transfiguration ties straight to the 1 Enoch 14 / 46 / 49 / 62 Son-of-Adam-in-glorified-form witness.$n$,$n$Matthew 17:2 — *And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light.* *But Peter and they that were with him were heavy with sleep: and when they were awake, they saw his glory, and the two men that stood with him.* (Luke 9:32) Matthew tells what the glory looked like, that *his face did shine as the sun* and his clothes were white as the light. Luke tells who saw it and in what state: Peter and the two with him were *heavy with sleep*, and *when they were awake, they saw his glory*, and the two men standing with him. They were not dreaming it; they woke into it.$n$),
(7030,9,54,$n$And when his disciples James and John saw this, they said, Lord, will you that we command fire to come down from heaven, and consume them, even as Elijah did?$n$,'matthew',10,14,$n$thread:shake-the-dust-off-rejection-response | The Samaritan village rejection — the disciples' *Lord, wilt thou that we command fire to come down from heaven, and consume them, even as Eliyahu (Elijah) did?* (Luke 9:54) is the wrong application of the Eliyahu (Elijah)-prophetic register. The King's rebuke — *the Son of Adam is not come to destroy men's lives, but to save them* (Luke 9:56) — names the seventy-sent-out posture of v.5: rejection means shaking-the-dust-off, not calling-fire-down.$n$,$n$Matthew 10:14 — *And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear your words, when ye depart out of that house or city, shake off the dust of your feet.* *And when his disciples James and John saw this, they said, Lord, will you that we command fire to come down from heaven, and consume them, even as Elijah did?* (Luke 9:54) A village will not take him in, and James and John ask for fire *as Elijah did*. Matthew has already said what a refused house is owed, to shake off the dust and go. Luke has him turn and rebuke them, saying *the Son of Adam is not come to destroy men’s lives, but to save them* (Luke 9:56), and they go to another village.$n$),
(7034,10,18,$n$And he said unto them, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven.$n$,'daniel',7,25,$n$thread:kingdoms-now-under-watcher-rebellion-administration | *I beheld ha-satan (Satan) as lightning fall from heaven.* The Watcher-rebellion / kingdoms-under-the-administration substance — the seventy's territory-by-territory undoing of the unclean-spirit administration IS the working-out of the lightning-fall the King saw.$n$,$n$Daniel 7:25 — *And he shall speak great words against the El Elyon (most High), and shall wear out the saints of the El Elyon (most High), and think to change times and laws: and they shall be given into his hand until a time and times and the dividing of time.* *And he said unto them, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven.* (Luke 10:18) The seventy come back glad that even the devils obey them, and Yahusha (Jesus) says he beheld Satan fall as lightning from heaven. Daniel's verse says what is let to run in the earth meanwhile: great words against the El Elyon (most High), the saints worn out, times and laws changed, for *a time and times and the dividing of time*. The next verse says the judgment sits and his dominion is taken away.$n$),
(7038,11,29,$n$And when the people were gathered thick together, he began to say, This is an evil generation: they seek a sign; and there shall no sign be given it, but the sign of Jonah the prophet.$n$,'matthew',12,39,$n$thread:sign-of-yonah-resurrection-credential | *No sign shall be given it, but the sign of Yonah (Jonah) the prophet.* Luke's version emphasizes the preaching-as-the-sign more than Matthew's three-days-three-nights emphasis, but the same Yonah-as-resurrection-credential substance.$n$,$n$Matthew 12:39 — *But he answered and said unto them, An evil and adulterous generation seeketh after a sign; and there shall no sign be given to it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah:* *And when the people were gathered thick together, he began to say, This is an evil generation: they seek a sign; and there shall no sign be given it, but the sign of Jonah the prophet.* (Luke 11:29) Matthew has the scribes and Pharisees ask for a sign and answers *An evil and adulterous generation seeketh after a sign*; Luke has the answer go out to a crowd *gathered thick together*. Luke goes on at once to say what Jonah was, *a sign unto the Ninevites* (Luke 11:30), and puts the weight on the preaching Nineveh repented at. Matthew's next verse adds the three days and three nights.$n$),
(7041,11,27,$n$And it came to pass, as he spake these things, a certain woman of the company lifted up her voice, and said unto him, Blessed is the womb that bare you, and the paps which you have sucked.$n$,'matthew',12,50,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | The same hearing-and-doing-reveals-the-family substance the King had named at Luke 8:21. Luke's twin pericope.$n$,$n$Matthew 12:50 — *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* *And it came to pass, as he spake these things, a certain woman of the company lifted up her voice, and said unto him, Blessed is the womb that bare you, and the paps which you have sucked.* (Luke 11:27) In Luke a woman in the crowd blesses the womb that bore him, and he answers *Yea rather, blessed are they that hear the word of Elohim (God), and keep it* (Luke 11:28). Matthew has his mother and brothers waiting outside and the same turn: the ones he names as *brother, and sister, and mother* are those who do the Father's will. The family is known by the hearing that keeps the word.$n$),
(7042,12,1,$n$In the mean time, when there were gathered together an innumerable multitude of people, insomuch that they trode one upon another, he began to say unto his disciples first of all, Beware you of the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy.$n$,'matthew',16,6,$n$thread:leaven-as-corruption-substance | *Beware ye of the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy.* Luke specifies the leaven as hypocrisy in plain words — the framework-substance of the Pharisaic-system-as-fermenting-corruption.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:6 — *Then Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Take heed and beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and of the Sadducees.* *In the mean time, when there were gathered together an innumerable multitude of people, insomuch that they trode one upon another, he began to say unto his disciples first of all, Beware you of the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy.* (Luke 12:1) Matthew has to stop and say he did not mean bread but *the doctrine of the Pharisees and of the Sadducees* (Matthew 16:12). Luke names it before anyone can mistake it, *which is hypocrisy*, and says it to his disciples first while a crowd presses so close they tread on one another. Leaven works unseen through the whole lump, and here it is a teaching that looks like one thing and is another.$n$),
(7044,12,10,$n$And whosoever shall speak a word against the Son of Adam, it shall be forgiven him: but unto him that blasphemeth against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) it shall not be forgiven.$n$,'matthew',12,31,$n$thread:blasphemy-against-the-ruach-warning | *Unto him that blasphemeth against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) it shall not be forgiven.* Luke's version preserves the warning the framework reads as the no-place-of-repentance for the hardened disposition.$n$,$n$Matthew 12:31 — *Wherefore I say unto you, All manner of sin and blasphemy shall be forgiven unto men: but the blasphemy against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) shall not be forgiven unto men.* *And whosoever shall speak a word against the Son of Adam, it shall be forgiven him: but unto him that blasphemeth against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) it shall not be forgiven.* (Luke 12:10) Matthew sets the saying where the Pharisees have just called his work the work of Beelzebub. Luke sets it among his disciples, after *he that denieth me before men shall be denied before the angels of Elohim (God)* (Luke 12:9) and before the promise that the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) will teach them what to say. In both, a word against the Son of Adam is forgiven and the blasphemy against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) is not.$n$),
(7048,12,47,$n$And that servant, which knew his lord’s will, and prepared not himself, neither did according to his will, shall be beaten with many stripes.$n$,'matthew',25,14,$n$thread:grace-and-the-opportunity-that-can-be-lost | *That servant, which knew his lord's will, and prepared not himself, neither did according to his will, shall be beaten with many stripes.* The framework's post-harvest-sifting substance — names get blotted out for lawlessness in the watching wheat. *Much given, much required* (Luke 12:48) is the standard.$n$,$n$Matthew 25:14 — *For the kingdom of heaven is as a man travelling into a far country, who called his own servants, and delivered unto them his goods.* *And that servant, which knew his lord’s will, and prepared not himself, neither did according to his will, shall be beaten with many stripes.* (Luke 12:47) Matthew's lord *called his own servants, and delivered unto them his goods*. Luke's servant is one of that kind, one who *knew his lord’s will* and did not make ready or do it, and he *shall be beaten with many stripes*. Luke adds the measure, *unto whomsoever much is given, of him shall be much required* (Luke 12:48). These are servants of the house, judged by what they did with what they were given.$n$),
(7054,13,20,$n$And again he said, Whereunto shall I liken the kingdom of Elohim (God)?$n$,'matthew',13,33,$n$thread:leaven-as-corruption-substance | *It is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of meal, till the whole was leavened* (Luke 13:20-21) — the framework reading of leaven across the gospels is the corruption-substance, not the kingdom-growth-substance the inherited pulpit teaches.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:33 — *Another parable spake he unto them; The kingdom of heaven is like unto leaven, which a woman took, and hid in three measures of meal, till the whole was leavened.* *And again he said, Whereunto shall I liken the kingdom of Elohim (God)?* (Luke 13:20) Luke asks *Whereunto shall I liken the kingdom of Elohim (God)?* and answers with a woman who hid leaven in meal *till the whole was leavened*, the same picture Matthew gives. Earlier Luke names what leaven is, *which is hypocrisy* (Luke 12:1): small, hidden, and spreading through the whole lump until nothing in it is left clean.$n$),
(7055,14,1,$n$And it came to pass, as he went into the house of one of the chief Pharisees to eat bread on the sabbath day, that they watched him.$n$,'mark',2,27,$n$thread:shabbat-controversy-heart-shabbat-vs-pharisaic-fence | The Shabbat-healing of the man with dropsy at the Pharisee-ruler's house. The King asks *Is it lawful to heal on the sabbath day?* (v.3) — they hold their peace. Same heart-shabbat the framework restores — the day-Yahuah-hallowed is for the people, not against the people.$n$,$n$Mark 2:27 — *And he said unto them, The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath:* *And it came to pass, as he went into the house of one of the chief Pharisees to eat bread on the sabbath day, that they watched him.* (Luke 14:1) Luke has him at table in a chief Pharisee's house on the Sabbath, and *they watched him* while a man with dropsy stood before him. He asks *Is it lawful to heal on the sabbath day?* (Luke 14:3), and *they held their peace* (Luke 14:4). Mark's answer to the same watching is *The sabbath was made for man*: the day was given for the people who keep it, and he keeps it by healing the man before him.$n$),
(7056,14,27,$n$And whosoever does not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple.$n$,'matthew',16,24,$n$thread:take-up-the-cross-discipleship-cost | *And whosoever doth not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple* (Luke 14:27) — the bearing-the-cross discipleship-cost the King teaches across the gospels.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:24 — *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* *And whosoever does not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple.* (Luke 14:27) Matthew gives the saying as an invitation, *If any man will come after me*; Luke gives the same cross as a warning, that whoever does not bear it and come after him *cannot be my disciple*. He says it when *there went great multitudes with him* (Luke 14:25), turning to tell the crowd what following costs.$n$),
(7057,14,26,$n$If any man come to me, and hate not his father, and mother, and wife, and children, and brethren, and sisters, yea, and his own life also, he cannot be my disciple.$n$,'mark',3,33,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | *If any man come to me, and hate not his father, and mother, and wife, and children, and brethren, and sisters, yea, and his own life also, he cannot be my disciple* (Luke 14:26) — the same kinship-redefinition Yahusha (Jesus) walks at Mark 3:33's *Who is my mother, or my brethren?* The Hebrew idiom for relative-preference, not literal-hatred.$n$,$n$Mark 3:33 — *And he answered them, saying, Who is my mother, or my brethren?* *If any man come to me, and hate not his father, and mother, and wife, and children, and brethren, and sisters, yea, and his own life also, he cannot be my disciple.* (Luke 14:26) Mark has him look at those sitting round him and ask *Who is my mother, or my brethren?* Luke gives the other half, telling the crowds that whoever comes and does not hate father and mother, wife and children, cannot be his disciple. Hate here is the old way of saying love the one less, and Mark's answer says who stands first: *whosoever shall do the will of Elohim (God), the same is my brother* (Mark 3:35).$n$),
(7061,15,18,$n$I will arise and go to my father, and will say unto him, Father, I have sinned against heaven, and before you,$n$,'ezekiel',18,21,$n$thread:grace-and-the-opportunity-that-can-be-lost | The prodigal's repentance-speech: *Father, I have sinned against heaven, and before thee, And am no more worthy to be called thy son: make me as one of thy hired servants* (vv.18-19). Read against Yechezk'el (Ezekiel) 18:21 (*But if the wicked will turn from all his sins... he shall surely live*) — the wilderness son returning carries the same architecture.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 18:21 — *But if the wicked will turn from all his sins that he hath committed, and keep all my statutes, and do that which is lawful and right, he shall surely live, he shall not die.* *I will arise and go to my father, and will say unto him, Father, I have sinned against heaven, and before you,* (Luke 15:18) The son in the far country says *I will arise and go to my father*, and Ezekiel's verse says what such a turning is worth: the wicked man who turns from all his sins and keeps the statutes *shall surely live*. The father says *this my son was dead, and is alive again* (Luke 15:24). Luke does not name the houses, but set beside the prophets the son who went into a far country and came home is the divorced house of Yashar'el (Israel) coming back from the nations.$n$),
(7064,16,14,$n$And the Pharisees also, who were covetous, heard all these things: and they derided him.$n$,'mark',7,13,$n$thread:pharisaic-traditions-make-the-commandment-of-none-effect | *And the Pharisees also, who were covetous, heard all these things: and they derided him. And he said unto them, Ye are they which justify yourselves before men; but Elohim (God) knoweth your hearts: for that which is highly esteemed among men is abomination in the sight of Elohim (God)* (Luke 16:14-15).$n$,$n$Mark 7:13 — *Making the word of Elohim (God) of none effect through your tradition, which ye have delivered: and many such like things do ye.* *And the Pharisees also, who were covetous, heard all these things: and they derided him.* (Luke 16:14) In Mark the elders' tradition lets a man call what he owes his father and mother a gift and do nothing more for them, and so the commandment is made *of none effect*. Luke says what lay under that kind of teaching: the Pharisees were *covetous*, and they *derided him*. He answers *Ye are they which justify yourselves before men; but Elohim (God) knoweth your hearts* (Luke 16:15).$n$),
(7067,16,19,$n$There was a certain rich man, which was clothed in purple and fine linen, and fared sumptuously every day:$n$,'matthew',13,24,$n$thread:wheat-and-tares-the-post-harvest-sifting-architecture | The rich-man-and-Lazaros parable. FRAMEWORK-MAJOR for the framework-honest sheol-and-resurrection register. Two men, two outcomes — Lazaros carried by the angels into Avraham's (Abraham's) bosom; the rich-man lifting up his eyes in torments. *And beside all this, between us and you there is a great gulf fixed.*$n$,$n$Matthew 13:24 — *Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:* *There was a certain rich man, which was clothed in purple and fine linen, and fared sumptuously every day:* (Luke 16:19) Matthew's field has the good seed and the enemy's tares growing side by side until the harvest. Luke sets two men together in much the same way, a rich man *clothed in purple and fine linen* and a beggar at his gate, and it is after both have died that they are parted: the beggar *carried by the angels into Abraham’s bosom* (Luke 16:22), the rich man in torments, and *a great gulf fixed* (Luke 16:26) between.$n$),
(7068,16,29,$n$Abraham says unto him, They have Moses and the prophets; let them hear them.$n$,'2-peter',3,15,$n$thread:scripture-twisting-and-the-harmonization-discipline | *They have Mosheh (Moses) and the prophets; let them hear them... If they hear not Mosheh (Moses) and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded, though one rose from the dead* (Luke 16:29, 31). The standing-test the King names: hear Torah and the prophets.$n$,$n$2 Peter 3:15 — *And account that the longsuffering of our Lord is salvation; even as our beloved brother Paul also according to the wisdom given unto him hath written unto you;* *Abraham says unto him, They have Moses and the prophets; let them hear them.* (Luke 16:29) Abraham says the brothers already have what they need, *Moses and the prophets; let them hear them*, and that no one risen from the dead would do more. Peter says of Paul's letters *some things hard to be understood, which they that are unlearned and unstable wrest, as they do also the other scriptures* (2 Peter 3:16). In both the writings are enough, and the harm comes from twisting them, not from lacking more.$n$),
(7074,17,34,$n$I tell you, in that night there shall be two men in one bed; the one shall be taken, and the other shall be left.$n$,'matthew',13,24,$n$thread:wheat-and-tares-the-post-harvest-sifting-architecture | *Two shall be in one bed; the one shall be taken, and the other shall be left.* The Lukan day-of-the-Son-of-Adam (son of man) teaching — the same post-harvest-sifting architecture: at the appointed hour, the wheat and the tares are separated where they stand.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:24 — *Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:* *I tell you, in that night there shall be two men in one bed; the one shall be taken, and the other shall be left.* (Luke 17:34) Luke's night has two men in one bed, and *the one shall be taken, and the other shall be left*, set right after the flood and the fire that carried the careless away. Matthew's field shows who the two are: good seed sown by the man, and tares sown among the wheat by his enemy, growing in the one field.$n$),
(7077,18,9,$n$And he spake this parable unto certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous, and despised others:$n$,'ezekiel',18,21,$n$thread:grace-and-the-opportunity-that-can-be-lost | The Pharisee-and-publican parable. The publican smites upon his breast, saying *Elohim (God) be merciful to me a sinner* — the contrite-spirit posture the framework names. Yechezk'el (Ezekiel) 18:21's repentance-from-sins architecture is the substance the publican walks.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 18:21 — *But if the wicked will turn from all his sins that he hath committed, and keep all my statutes, and do that which is lawful and right, he shall surely live, he shall not die.* *And he spake this parable unto certain which trusted in themselves that they were righteous, and despised others:* (Luke 18:9) Luke aims the parable at men who *trusted in themselves that they were righteous*, men with nothing in their own eyes to turn from. Ezekiel's verse speaks to the other kind, the wicked man who turns from all his sins, keeps the statutes, and does what is lawful and right, and *he shall surely live*. The publican who smote his breast is that man turning.$n$),
(7083,18,31,$n$Then he took unto him the twelve, and said unto them, Behold, we go up to Jerusalem, and all things that are written by the prophets concerning the Son of Adam shall be accomplished.$n$,'matthew',16,21,$n$thread:first-passion-prediction-and-the-must-of-the-passion | The third passion-prediction at Luke 18:31-33. *Behold, we go up to Yerushalayim (Jerusalem), and all things that are written by the prophets concerning the Son of Adam (son of man) shall be accomplished. For he shall be delivered unto the Gentiles, and shall be mocked, and spitefully entreated.*$n$,$n$Matthew 16:21 — *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* *Then he took unto him the twelve, and said unto them, Behold, we go up to Jerusalem, and all things that are written by the prophets concerning the Son of Adam shall be accomplished.* (Luke 18:31) Matthew has Yahusha (Jesus) telling his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer, and has Peter turn it aside. Luke adds that he *took unto him the twelve* on the road and told them that *all things that are written by the prophets* about the Son of Adam would be accomplished, so the suffering was already written. Luke says plainly that they understood none of it.$n$),
(7084,18,35,$n$And it came to pass, that as he was come nigh unto Jericho, a certain blind man sat by the way side begging:$n$,'matthew',9,27,$n$thread:son-of-david-messianic-recognition-by-marginalized | The blind man at Yericho (Jericho). *Yahusha (Jesus), thou Son of David, have mercy on me* (Luke 18:38). The Son-of-David Messianic-recognition cry from the marginalized — the same pattern the framework names across the gospels.$n$,$n$Matthew 9:27 — *And when Yahusha (Jesus) departed thence, two blind men followed him, crying, and saying, Thou Son of David, have mercy on us.* *And it came to pass, that as he was come nigh unto Jericho, a certain blind man sat by the way side begging:* (Luke 18:35) Matthew has two blind men following him and crying *Thou Son of David, have mercy on us*. Luke gives the one at Jericho, who *sat by the way side begging*, heard the crowd pass, and learned it was Yahusha (Jesus) of Nazareth. He cries out the same title, and the crowd that told him to hold his peace had not called Yahusha (Jesus) that.$n$),
(7085,18,42,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, Receive your sight: your faith has saved you.$n$,'acts',3,6,$n$thread:messiah-as-torah-affirming-healer | *Receive thy sight: thy faith hath saved thee. And immediately he received his sight, and followed him, glorifying Elohim (God): and all the people, when they saw it, gave praise unto Elohim (God)* (Luke 18:42-43).$n$,$n$Acts 3:6 — *Then Peter said, Silver and gold have I none; but such as I have give I thee: In the name of Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ) of Nazareth rise up and walk.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, Receive your sight: your faith has saved you.* (Luke 18:42) A beggar sits by the road in Luke and a lame man sits at the temple gate in Acts, and both are given the body they lacked. Yahusha (Jesus) says *thy faith hath saved thee* to the blind man, who follows him praising Elohim (God). Peter speaks in the same name, and the lame man walks into the temple praising Elohim (God).$n$),
(7087,19,10,$n$For the Son of Adam is come to seek and to save that which was lost.$n$,'matthew',10,6,$n$thread:lost-sheep-mission-scope | The King's standing programmatic statement at Zakkay's (Zacchaeus's) house — *the Son of Adam is come to seek and to save that which was lost* — names the same mission scope Matt 10:6 and 15:24 carry: the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel). The seeking-and-saving is the gathering-of-the-scattered, not categorical-nations inclusion.$n$,$n$Matthew 10:6 — *But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* *For the Son of Adam is come to seek and to save that which was lost.* (Luke 19:10) Matthew has Yahusha (Jesus) sending the twelve to *the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)*, and Luke has him say at Zacchaeus's table that the Son of Adam came *to seek and to save that which was lost*. They are the same lost ones, now sought by the one who sent the twelve out. The man at the table, a publican, is a son of Abraham by Luke's own word, one of the house.$n$),
(177457,21,24,$n$And they shall fall by the edge of the sword, and shall be led away captive into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down of the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.$n$,'ezekiel',9,6,$n$*...but come not near any man upon whom is the mark; and begin at my sanctuary* (Ezekiel 9:6). The treading-down of Luke 21:24 is the same separation Ezekiel saw, and it starts inside the house: the mark divides the sighing remnant from the abominations they grieved, and the dividing begins at the holy place. The counterfeit sown into the assembly is burned out first.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 9:6 — *Slay utterly old and young, both maids, and little children, and women: but come not near any man upon whom is the mark; and begin at my sanctuary. Then they began at the ancient men which were before the house.* *And they shall fall by the edge of the sword, and shall be led away captive into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down of the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.* (Luke 21:24) Luke's Jerusalem *trodden down* is a judgment on the city, and Ezekiel shows how such a judgment goes. The slayers are told to *come not near any man upon whom is the mark*, the men who sigh and cry over the abominations, and to *begin at my sanctuary*. It starts at the house of Elohim (God), and the marked are spared.$n$),
(177458,21,24,$n$And they shall fall by the edge of the sword, and shall be led away captive into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down of the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.$n$,'zechariah',13,8,$n$*...two parts therein shall be cut off and die; but the third shall be left therein* (Zechariah 13:8). The desolation Luke names is a sifting, not an erasure: two parts cut off, a third brought through the fire and refined as silver. The treading-down purges the field; the remnant it leaves is the silver Yahuah (the LORD) calls *my people* (Zechariah 13:9).$n$,$n$Zechariah 13:8 — *And it shall come to pass, that in all the land, saith Yahuah (LORD), two parts therein shall be cut off and die; but the third shall be left therein.* *And they shall fall by the edge of the sword, and shall be led away captive into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down of the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.* (Luke 21:24) Luke has the sword and the captivity into all nations, and Zechariah says what such a cutting leaves behind. Two parts are *cut off and die*, but *the third shall be left therein*. That third is brought through the fire and refined like silver, and Yahuah (the LORD) calls them his people, so this is a sifting and not an end.$n$),
(177459,21,24,$n$And they shall fall by the edge of the sword, and shall be led away captive into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down of the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.$n$,'zechariah',14,9,$n$*And Yahuah (the LORD) shall be king over all the earth: in that day shall there be one Yahuah (the LORD), and his name one* (Zechariah 14:9). Where the times of the Gentiles are fulfilled (Luke 21:24), the day of Yahuah closes: all nations gathered against the city, his feet on the mount of Olives, and the King over all the earth. The desolation runs until the kingdom comes.$n$,$n$Zechariah 14:9 — *And Yahuah (LORD) shall be king over all the earth: in that day shall there be one Yahuah (LORD), and his name one.* *And they shall fall by the edge of the sword, and shall be led away captive into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down of the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.* (Luke 21:24) Luke puts an *until* on the treading down, *until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled*. Zechariah's day closes with *Yahuah (LORD) shall be king over all the earth*, one Yahuah (the LORD) and his name one, so the city that is trampled is not left to the nations for good.$n$),
(177462,21,24,$n$And they shall fall by the edge of the sword, and shall be led away captive into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down of the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.$n$,'ezekiel',33,6,$n$*But if the watchman see the sword come, and blow not the trumpet, and the people be not warned... his blood will I require at the watchman's hand* (Ezekiel 33:6). When ye see Jerusalem compassed with armies (Luke 21:20), the sword Ezekiel's watchman was set to warn of has come. The treading-down of Luke 21:24 is the desolation the watchman was posted for; the trumpet is the warning the remnant hears before the field is cleared.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 33:6 — *But if the watchman see the sword come, and blow not the trumpet, and the people be not warned; if the sword come, and take any person from among them, he is taken away in his iniquity; but his blood will I require at the watchman’s hand.* *And they shall fall by the edge of the sword, and shall be led away captive into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down of the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.* (Luke 21:24) Luke's *edge of the sword* is the sword Ezekiel's watchman is posted to watch for. If he sees it come and *blow not the trumpet*, the one taken is taken away in his iniquity and *his blood will I require at the watchman’s hand*. Yahusha (Jesus) telling his disciples beforehand is the trumpet blown.$n$),
(177463,21,24,$n$And they shall fall by the edge of the sword, and shall be led away captive into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down of the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.$n$,'ezekiel',33,28,$n$*For I will lay the land most desolate... because of all their abominations which they have committed* (Ezekiel 33:28-29). Jerusalem trodden down of the Gentiles (Luke 21:24) is the same desolation Ezekiel named — the land laid most desolate for the abominations done in it. The treading-down is not random ruin; it is the judgment Ezekiel saw, clearing the field before the scattered seed is gathered home.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 33:28 — *For I will lay the land most desolate, and the pomp of her strength shall cease; and the mountains of Yashar'el (Israel) shall be desolate, that none shall pass through.* *And they shall fall by the edge of the sword, and shall be led away captive into all nations: and Jerusalem shall be trodden down of the Gentiles, until the times of the Gentiles be fulfilled.* (Luke 21:24) Luke has the people *led away captive into all nations* and Jerusalem *trodden down*, and Ezekiel says what such a laying waste answers: *I will lay the land most desolate*, with the mountains of Yashar'el (Israel) desolate so that none pass through. The next verse gives the reason, all their abominations, so the ruin answers what was done in the land.$n$);
INSERT INTO _s496_l1_t VALUES
('luke-1-the-throne-of-his-father-david-and-the-kingdom-without-end',$n$The throne of his father David and the kingdom without end$n$,$n$It ain't new. Gabriel does not hand Mary a fresh promise. He hands her the oldest one David ever heard and says it was always for a son.
INSERT INTO _s496_l1_a VALUES
('beliar-as-king-of-this-world','matthew',4,8,10);
INSERT INTO _s496_l1_m VALUES
('gather-together-in-one-the-children-scattered-abroad',6956,1017,$n$Malachi 4:5 — *Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of Yahuah (LORD):* Malachi promises Elijah the prophet before the great and dreadful day, and Gabriel tells Zacharias that John comes in his spirit and power.$n$),
('luke-1-the-throne-of-his-father-david-and-the-kingdom-without-end',6958,1031,$n$2 Samuel 7:12 — *I will set up thy seed after thee, which shall proceed out of thy bowels.* David is promised a seed out of his own body, one son whose kingdom Yahuah (the LORD) will establish, and Gabriel says Mary will bear him.$n$),
('luke-1-the-throne-of-his-father-david-and-the-kingdom-without-end',6959,1032,$n$2 Samuel 7:12 — *which shall proceed out of thy bowels, and I will establish his kingdom.* The kingdom Yahuah (the LORD) promised David is the one Gabriel says Mary's son will receive, on his father David's throne.$n$),
('luke-1-the-throne-of-his-father-david-and-the-kingdom-without-end',6960,1033,$n$Daniel 7:14 — *his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away.* Daniel sees an everlasting dominion that shall not pass away and a kingdom that shall not be destroyed, which is the kingdom Gabriel says has no end.$n$),
('luke-1-the-magnificat-gathering-of-the-tribes-of-jacob-in-sirach-36-and-baruch',6964,1055,$n$Genesis 17:7 — *for an everlasting covenant, to be a Elohim (God) unto thee, and to thy seed after thee.* The covenant is everlasting and runs to Abraham and to his seed after him, which is the oath Mary says is being kept.$n$),
('benedictus-as-priestly-witness-to-the-davidic-horn-and-the-avraham-oath',6965,1067,$n$Isaiah 11:1 — *And there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots:* A rod comes out of the stem of Jesse, David's father, and a Branch from his roots, which is the horn Zacharias names.$n$),
('luke-2-the-heavenly-host-praising-before-yahuah-of-spirits-in-1-enoch',6970,2013,$n$Revelation 19:1 — *I heard a great voice of much people in heaven, saying, Alleluia.* A great voice of much people in heaven gives salvation, glory, honour and power to Elohim (God), as the host did at the birth.$n$),
('luke-3-political-and-priestly-backdrop-as-the-prophetic-clock',6973,3002,$n$Isaiah 40:3 — *The voice of him that crieth in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of Yahuah (LORD).* Isaiah's voice crying in the wilderness is the place and the work Luke gives John when the word of Elohim (God) comes to him there.$n$),
('matthew-3-fruits-meet-for-repentance-stones-to-abraham-isaiah-1-ezekiel-18-romans-11',6976,3008,$n$Matthew 3:9 — *And think not to say within yourselves, We have Abraham to our father: for I say unto you, that Elohim (God) is able of these stones to raise up children unto Abraham.* John tells them not to rest on having Abraham as father, because Elohim (God) is able of these stones to raise up children unto Abraham.$n$),
('beliar-as-king-of-this-world',6984,4005,$n$Matthew 4:8 — *Again, the devil taketh him up into an exceeding high mountain, and sheweth him all the kingdoms of the world, and the glory of them;* Matthew's mountain is exceeding high and the devil shows all the kingdoms and their glory, which Luke has him say are delivered to him.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',6987,4025,$n$Matthew 15:24 — *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* The errand Matthew states is the one Luke has him name at Nazareth, with the widow of Sidon and Naaman as sheep who heard the prophet.$n$),
('luke-4-i-must-preach-the-kingdom-for-therefore-am-i-sent-isaiah-61-and-52',6989,4043,$n$Matthew 15:24 — *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* Matthew gives the boundary of his errand, the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), which is what Luke's therefore am I sent points to.$n$),
('luke-5-shew-thyself-to-the-priest-as-moses-commanded-leviticus-13-14-2-kings-5',6991,5014,$n$Matthew 8:4 — *shew thyself to the priest, and offer the gift that Moses commanded, for a testimony unto them.* Matthew's Yahusha (Jesus) sends the healed leper to the priest with the gift Moses commanded, as a testimony to them.$n$),
('luke-5-the-bridegroom-and-the-new-wine-joel-2-isaiah-62-jeremiah-31',6994,5034,$n$Matthew 9:15 — *but the days will come, when the bridegroom shall be taken from them, and then shall they fast.* Matthew asks whether the guests can mourn while the bridegroom is with them, then says the days will come when he is taken and they fast.$n$),
('luke-5-the-bridegroom-and-the-new-wine-joel-2-isaiah-62-jeremiah-31',6995,5036,$n$Matthew 9:17 — *but they put new wine into new bottles, and both are preserved.* Matthew's wine and bottles carry the same lesson as Luke's cloth: the new is not put into what cannot hold it, and both are preserved.$n$),
('luke-6-the-son-of-adam-is-lord-of-the-sabbath-deuteronomy-23-1-samuel-21',6996,6001,$n$Mark 2:23 — *he went through the corn fields on the sabbath day.* Mark says it was the sabbath day and that the disciples began to pluck the ears as they went, which Luke sets on the second sabbath after the first.$n$),
('luke-6-is-it-lawful-to-do-good-on-the-sabbath-deuteronomy-22-isaiah-58',6997,6006,$n$Mark 3:1 — *And he entered again into the synagogue; and there was a man there which had a withered hand.* Mark puts him again in the synagogue with a man whose hand was withered, and the question of the sabbath follows.$n$),
('luke-6-he-chose-twelve-the-tribes-regathered-genesis-49-exodus-24',6998,6012,$n$Matthew 10:1 — *he gave them power against unclean spirits, to cast them out.* Matthew has the twelve called and given power to cast out unclean spirits and heal, and Luke shows the night of prayer before the choosing.$n$),
('matthew-8-many-from-east-and-west-sit-with-abraham-isaiah-25-malachi-1',7006,7009,$n$Matthew 8:10 — *I have not found so great faith, no, not in Yashar'el (Israel).* Matthew gives the same verdict on the centurion's faith, spoken to those who followed, and sets it before many coming from east and west.$n$),
('matthew-8-many-from-east-and-west-sit-with-abraham-isaiah-25-malachi-1',7007,7001,$n$Matthew 8:5 — *And when Yahusha (Jesus) was entered into Capernaum, there came unto him a centurion, beseeching him,* Matthew has the centurion come to him himself at Capernaum, beseeching him, where Luke has him send the elders of the Yahudim (Jews) and then friends.$n$),
('matthew-11-john-the-messenger-elijah-restore-the-tribes-malachi-3-malachi-4-sirach-48',7008,7027,$n$Malachi 3:1 — *Behold, I will send my messenger, and he shall prepare the way before me.* Malachi's messenger prepares the way before Yahuah (the Lord), and Yahusha (Jesus) names John as that messenger.$n$),
('matthew-11-john-the-messenger-elijah-restore-the-tribes-malachi-3-malachi-4-sirach-48',7009,7028,$n$Matthew 11:11 — *there hath not risen a greater than John the Baptist.* Matthew has the same verdict on John and the same turn to the least in the kingdom, and the verses after it name him as Elijah.$n$),
('my-sheep-hear-my-voice-hearing-reveals-not-creates',7010,7029,$n$John 10:27 — *My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me:* The sheep hear his voice and he knows them, and hearing is how they are shown to be his; Luke's publicans hear John in the same way.$n$),
('matthew-11-john-the-messenger-elijah-restore-the-tribes-malachi-3-malachi-4-sirach-48',7011,7033,$n$1 Kings 19:8 — *went in the strength of that meat forty days and forty nights unto Horeb the mount of Elohim (God).* Elijah ate and drank and walked forty days and nights on the strength of that meal to Horeb, the mount of Elohim (God), as John lived in the wilderness.$n$),
('matthew-11-john-the-messenger-elijah-restore-the-tribes-malachi-3-malachi-4-sirach-48',7012,7024,$n$1 Kings 19:10 — *for the children of Yashar'el (Israel) have forsaken thy covenant, thrown down thine altars, and slain thy prophets with the sword.* Elijah tells Yahuah (the LORD) the children of Yashar'el (Israel) have forsaken the covenant and slain the prophets, which is the lot of the prophet John stands in.$n$),
('luke-8-the-word-sown-and-the-law-bringing-fruit-in-2-esdras',7014,8004,$n$Matthew 13:3 — *And he spake many things unto them in parables, saying, Behold, a sower went forth to sow;* Matthew gives the same sower going forth to sow, spoken among many parables, where Luke names the crowd from every city.$n$),
('matthew-13-the-sower-why-parables-seeing-they-see-not-isaiah-6-isaiah-55',7015,8010,$n$Matthew 13:11 — *He answered and said unto them, Because it is given unto you to know the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven, but to them it is not given.* Matthew says plainly that it is given to them to know the mysteries of the kingdom and to the others it is not given.$n$),
('matthew-12-whosoever-shall-do-the-will-of-my-father-the-true-family-deuteronomy-33-psalm-22',7017,8021,$n$Matthew 12:50 — *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* Matthew says whoever does the will of his Father in heaven is his brother, sister and mother, the same household Luke names by hearing and doing.$n$),
('matthew-10-shake-off-the-dust-and-sodom',7022,9005,$n$Matthew 10:14 — *And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear your words, when ye depart out of that house or city, shake off the dust of your feet.* Matthew names the refusal as not receiving the twelve and not hearing their words, and has them shake off the dust as they leave.$n$),
('luke-9-feeding-the-five-thousand-the-shepherd-feeds-the-flock-2-kings-4-exodus-16',7023,9010,$n$Matthew 14:13 — *and when the people had heard thereof, they followed him on foot out of the cities.* Matthew says why he withdrew, having heard of John, and that the crowds came after him on foot out of the cities.$n$),
('luke-9-the-messiah-of-elohim-confessed-and-the-suffering-foretold-psalm-2-isaiah-53',7024,9018,$n$Matthew 16:13 — *he asked his disciples, saying, Whom do men say that I the Son of Adam am?* Matthew sets the question at Caesarea Philippi and has Yahusha (Jesus) name himself in it as the Son of Adam.$n$),
('luke-9-the-messiah-of-elohim-confessed-and-the-suffering-foretold-psalm-2-isaiah-53',7025,9021,$n$Matthew 16:21 — *how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes.* Matthew marks the turn at that time and says he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders, chief priests and scribes.$n$),
('luke-9-take-up-thy-cross-and-the-glory-of-the-son-of-adam-daniel-7-psalm-49',7026,9023,$n$Matthew 16:24 — *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* Matthew gives the saying to his disciples without the word daily: deny himself, take up his cross, and follow.$n$),
('luke-9-the-transfiguration-moses-and-elijah-hear-him-deuteronomy-18-exodus-34-malachi-4',7027,9028,$n$Matthew 17:1 — *And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) taketh Peter, James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into an high mountain apart,* Matthew counts six days and takes Peter, James and John his brother up an high mountain apart, without saying they went to pray.$n$),
('luke-9-the-transfiguration-moses-and-elijah-hear-him-deuteronomy-18-exodus-34-malachi-4',7029,9032,$n$Matthew 17:2 — *And was transfigured before them: and his face did shine as the sun, and his raiment was white as the light.* Matthew says what the transfiguration looked like: his face shining as the sun and his clothing white as the light.$n$),
('luke-9-he-set-his-face-to-go-to-jerusalem-the-undivided-call-isaiah-50-2-kings-1-1-kings-19',7030,9054,$n$Matthew 10:14 — *And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear your words, when ye depart out of that house or city, shake off the dust of your feet.* Matthew gives the answer to a place that will not receive the twelve: they shake off the dust of their feet and leave.$n$),
('luke-10-satan-fallen-as-lightning-and-the-watcher-fall-in-1-enoch-and-wisdom',7034,10018,$n$Daniel 7:25 — *shall wear out the saints of the El Elyon (most High), and think to change times and laws.* Daniel's verse says what is let to run for a set time: great words against the El Elyon (most High), the saints worn out, times and laws changed.$n$),
('luke-11-the-sign-of-jonah-and-the-queen-of-the-south-jonah-1-1-kings-10',7038,11029,$n$Matthew 12:39 — *there shall no sign be given to it, but the sign of the prophet Jonah.* Matthew gives the same answer to the scribes and Pharisees who asked for a sign, and calls the generation adulterous as well as evil.$n$),
('matthew-12-whosoever-shall-do-the-will-of-my-father-the-true-family-deuteronomy-33-psalm-22',7041,11027,$n$Matthew 12:50 — *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* Matthew gives the same answer, naming as his brother and sister and mother whoever does the will of his Father in heaven.$n$),
('matthew-16-beware-the-leaven-of-the-pharisees-and-sadducees-exodus-12-1-corinthians-5',7042,12001,$n$Matthew 16:6 — *Then Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Take heed and beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and of the Sadducees.* Matthew gives the warning against the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees, which the disciples first take for talk about bread.$n$),
('matthew-12-binding-the-strong-man-blasphemy-against-the-spirit-isaiah-49-1-enoch-15',7044,12010,$n$Matthew 12:31 — *Wherefore I say unto you, All manner of sin and blasphemy shall be forgiven unto men: but the blasphemy against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) shall not be forgiven unto men.* Matthew gives the saying that every sin and blasphemy shall be forgiven men except blasphemy against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit).$n$),
('the-diligent-stewardship-versus-the-slothful-servant-in-sirach',7048,12047,$n$Matthew 25:14 — *For the kingdom of heaven is as a man travelling into a far country, who called his own servants, and delivered unto them his goods.* Matthew shows the lord going into a far country and putting his goods into the hands of his own servants.$n$),
('matthew-13-the-mustard-seed-the-leaven-the-birds-lodge-ezekiel-17-daniel-4',7054,13020,$n$Matthew 13:33 — *like unto leaven, which a woman took, and hid in three measures of meal, till the whole was leavened.* Matthew gives the same picture, the woman, the three measures of meal, and the leaven working until the whole was leavened.$n$),
('mark-2-the-sabbath-was-made-for-man-the-son-of-adam-lord-of-the-sabbath-genesis-2-exodus-20-jubilees-2',7055,14001,$n$Mark 2:27 — *And he said unto them, The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath:* Mark gives the saying itself, that the sabbath was made for man and not man for the sabbath, which Luke's scene acts out at the table.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',7056,14027,$n$Matthew 16:24 — *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* Matthew gives the saying as an invitation to his disciples: deny himself, take up his cross, and follow.$n$),
('count-the-cost-tower-builder-king-going-to-war-and-the-disciple-who-forsakes-not-all-he-hath',7057,14026,$n$Mark 3:33 — *And he answered them, saying, Who is my mother, or my brethren?* Mark gives the question about his mother and brethren, whose answer names the household Luke's saying sets above father and mother.$n$),
('prodigal-son-and-the-elder-brother-the-two-house-gathering-of-ephraim-and-yahudah',7061,15018,$n$Ezekiel 18:21 — *the wicked will turn from all his sins that he hath committed, and keep all my statutes.* Ezekiel says the wicked man who turns from all his sins and keeps the statutes shall surely live and not die.$n$),
('mark-7-the-corban-tradition-voids-the-fifth-commandment-in-sirach',7064,16014,$n$Mark 7:13 — *Making the word of Elohim (God) of none effect through your tradition, which ye have delivered: and many such like things do ye.* Mark gives the verdict that the tradition makes the word of Elohim (God) of none effect, and says many such things were done.$n$),
('rich-man-and-lazaros-the-framework-honest-sheol-with-two-compartments-and-the-mosheh-and-the-prophets-test',7067,16019,$n$Matthew 13:24 — *Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:* Matthew shows a man sowing good seed in his field, the ground where wheat and tares will grow together until the harvest.$n$),
('rich-man-and-lazaros-the-framework-honest-sheol-with-two-compartments-and-the-mosheh-and-the-prophets-test',7068,16029,$n$2 Peter 3:15 — *even as our beloved brother Paul also according to the wisdom given unto him hath written unto you.* Peter points his readers to Paul's letters and in the next verse warns that the unstable twist what is hard in them as they do the other scriptures.$n$),
('matthew-13-the-tares-the-harvest-is-the-end-of-the-world-daniel-12-malachi-4',7074,17034,$n$Matthew 13:24 — *Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:* Matthew's field holds two seeds, good seed and tares, growing in one place, which is the picture behind two in one bed and only one taken.$n$),
('pharisee-and-publican-the-flesh-credential-vs-mercy-contrast-and-the-judaizer-architecture-in-parable-form',7077,18009,$n$Ezekiel 18:21 — *turn from all his sins that he hath committed, and keep all my statutes.* Ezekiel says the wicked man who turns from all his sins and keeps the statutes shall surely live, which is the turning the publican does.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',7083,18031,$n$Matthew 16:21 — *how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes.* Matthew names who does the suffering to him, the elders and chief priests and scribes, and that he will be raised again the third day.$n$),
('matthew-9-two-blind-men-thou-son-of-david-isaiah-35-psalm-146-2-samuel-7',7084,18035,$n$Matthew 9:27 — *And when Yahusha (Jesus) departed thence, two blind men followed him, crying, and saying, Thou Son of David, have mercy on us.* Two blind men follow him crying Thou Son of David, have mercy on us, the same title Luke's beggar cries out at Jericho.$n$),
('luke-18-all-that-the-prophets-wrote-fulfilled-and-the-son-of-david-isaiah-53-2-samuel-7',7085,18042,$n$Acts 3:6 — *In the name of Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ) of Nazareth rise up and walk.* Peter heals the lame man at the gate in the name of Yahusha (Jesus), and the man walks into the temple praising Elohim (God).$n$),
('matthew-10-the-lost-sheep-of-the-house-of-israel-ezekiel-34-jeremiah-50',7087,19010,$n$Matthew 10:6 — *But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* Matthew names the lost as the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel), which tells who the Son of Adam came to seek.$n$),
('luke-21-jerusalem-trodden-down-and-the-day-of-yahuah-zechariah-14',177457,21024,$n$Ezekiel 9:6 — *come not near any man upon whom is the mark; and begin at my sanctuary.* The slaying in Jerusalem begins at the sanctuary and passes over the men marked for sighing over the abominations.$n$),
('luke-21-jerusalem-trodden-down-and-the-day-of-yahuah-zechariah-14',177458,21024,$n$Zechariah 13:8 — *two parts therein shall be cut off and die; but the third shall be left therein.* Two parts of the land are cut off and die, and a third is left to be brought through the fire and refined.$n$),
('luke-21-jerusalem-trodden-down-and-the-day-of-yahuah-zechariah-14',177459,21024,$n$Zechariah 14:9 — *And Yahuah (LORD) shall be king over all the earth: in that day shall there be one Yahuah (LORD), and his name one.* On the day Zechariah describes, Yahuah (the LORD) is king over all the earth, one Yahuah (the LORD) with one name.$n$),
('luke-21-jerusalem-trodden-down-and-the-day-of-yahuah-zechariah-14',177462,21024,$n$Ezekiel 33:6 — *if the sword come, and take any person from among them, he is taken away in his iniquity.* The watchman who sees the sword and does not blow the trumpet answers for the blood of those taken away in their iniquity.$n$),
('luke-21-jerusalem-trodden-down-and-the-day-of-yahuah-zechariah-14',177463,21024,$n$Ezekiel 33:28 — *I will lay the land most desolate, and the pomp of her strength shall cease.* Ezekiel says Yahuah (the LORD) lays the land most desolate because of the abominations done in it.$n$);
